  "posts": [
    {
      "slug": "what-we-learned-in-the-quayside-lobby",
      "title": "What we learned in the Quayside lobby",
      "date": "2026-11-12",
      "categories": [
        "pilots"
      ],
      "tags": [
        "lobby",
        "front-desk"
      ],
      "image": "assets/images/demo/pilot-quayside.webp",
      "excerpt": "Rosa Almeida describes the mornings at the Quayside Works front desk before and after the pilot panel.",
      "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At Quayside Works the day used to start at the front desk with the same question. Rosa Almeida, who looks after reception across three floors and 240 desks, was asked whether anywhere was free dozens of times before ten o'clock.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>With the pilot panel on the lobby wall, people look at the wall first. Rosa still answers questions, but they are different ones now, about where the quiet desks are rather than whether there are any.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
    },
    {
      "slug": "why-a-held-desk-lets-go-after-fifteen-minutes",
      "title": "Why a held desk lets go after fifteen minutes",
      "date": "2026-11-04",
      "categories": [
        "product"
      ],
      "tags": [
        "desk-tags",
        "design"
      ],
      "image": "assets/images/demo/hold-release.webp",
      "excerpt": "Mara and Kwame on why a hold lasts fifteen minutes and then lets go by itself.",
      "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A held desk turns clay on the panel and stays held for fifteen minutes. Mara Lindqvist and Kwame Asante chose that time after watching how long people really take to cross a floor with a bag and a coffee.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If nobody taps the desk tag in that time, the hold lets go by itself and the desk turns sage again. Nobody has to cancel, and no desk stays reserved for someone who never arrived.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
    }
